<text x="311" y="88" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f5a623">−7%</text>
<rect x="210" y="96" width="333" height="18" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/>
<text x="551" y="110" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">−25%</text>
<text x="20" y="162" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">Insert, 1 client</text>
<rect x="210" y="136" width="67" height="18" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/>
<text x="285" y="150" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f5a623">−5%</text>
<rect x="210" y="158" width="160" height="18" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/>
<text x="378" y="172" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">−12%</text>
<text x="20" y="224" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">Insert, 32 clients</text>
<rect x="210" y="198" width="80" height="18" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/>
<text x="298" y="212" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f5a623">−6%</text>
<rect x="210" y="220" width="347" height="18" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/>
<text x="565" y="234" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">−26%</text>
<text x="210" y="260" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#6b746c" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
<text x="343" y="260" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#6b746c" text-anchor="middle">10%</text>
<text x="477" y="260" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#6b746c" text-anchor="middle">20%</text>
<text x="610" y="260" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#6b746c" text-anchor="middle">30%</text>
<text x="20" y="284" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">Bars share one scale starting at 0. Medians of three 20-second runs; SoliDB's 6% at 32 clients</text>
<text x="20" y="302" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">is inside that series' noise. Updates that leave the reference alone: no measurable cost for SoliDB.</text>
</svg><figcaption>A foreign key costs SoliDB 5 to 7% on the writes that set the reference, and PostgreSQL 12 to 26%: one read of the parent under a database-wide shared lock, against a row lock and a trigger per row.</figcaption></figure>
<p>That shared lock is the other side. A delete in a referenced collection takes it alone: it waits for the writes in progress on the linked collections of its database and holds the others back while it runs, where PostgreSQL locks only the rows involved. We have not measured a workload that deletes parents while it inserts children; one that does so all day will feel it, one that mostly inserts and updates will not.</p>
</section>

<section id="edges"><h2>Edges</h2>
<p>A graph has the same problem with a different shape. An edge's <code>_from</code> and <code>_to</code> hold the <code>_id</code> of a vertex, and nothing checked that the vertex existed, or removed the edge when it went. A key can refer by <code>_id</code>, in both of its forms (<code>customers/ann</code> and <code>shop:customers/ann</code>), and that is the default for an edge's two ends:</p>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> http</div><pre>POST /_api/database/shop/collection/follows/foreign-keys
{"field": "_from", "references": "customers", "on_delete": "cascade"}

{"name": "fk_from", "field": "_from", "by": "id", "references": "customers",
 "on_delete": "cascade", "index": "_edge_from_idx"}</pre></div>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> sdbql</div><pre><span class="tok-kw">INSERT</span> {_from: <span class="tok-str">"customers/ann"</span>, _to: <span class="tok-str">"customers/zed"</span>} <span class="tok-kw">INTO</span> follows

409  Foreign key violation: '_to' in follows refers to 'zed',
     which is not a document of 'customers'</pre></div>
<p>With <code>cascade</code> on both ends, deleting a customer deletes the edges that touch it, in the same write. The key uses the two indexes every edge collection already has.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits"><h2>Where it stops</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Not on sharded collections.</strong> A reference is checked against the documents of one node. On a node that replicates, the node that takes a write checks it and the others apply it as written; what a delete cascades into is replicated as writes of its own. Two nodes that accept conflicting writes at the same moment can still leave an orphan, which the check endpoint finds.</li>
<li><strong>A key names one collection.</strong> An edge collection that links vertices of several collections cannot declare one on that end.</li>
<li><strong>Most clients do not know about it yet.</strong> The HTTP endpoints, the driver's commands, the Rust client and the admin console do. The other client libraries have no method for it, and the Soli ORM's <code>belongs_to</code> still does its own work instead of declaring a key.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>solidb-dump</code> writes the keys and <code>solidb-restore</code> declares them after the data, so a dump restores in any order. The reference is in <a href="/docs/foreign-keys">the foreign keys guide</a>.</p>
</section>
</div>
